<Docs slug="getting-started">
  <h1>Getting started</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Ouijit is a task and terminal manager for running coding agents in parallel. Each task gets its own git
    worktree and terminal, lifecycle hooks launch your agent with the right context, and a session-aware CLI lets
    agents drive the board back. Install it and start your first task in a minute.
  </p>

  <h2>Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>macOS</strong> 13+ (Apple Silicon or Intel) or <strong>Linux</strong> x64</li>
    <li><strong>Git</strong> 2.20+ on <code>PATH</code></li>
  </ul>
  <p>The downloads are self-contained. Nothing else is required to run the app.</p>

  <h2>Download and install</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/ouijit/ouijit/releases/latest/download/ouijit-darwin-arm64.dmg">macOS (Apple Silicon)</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/ouijit/ouijit/releases/latest/download/ouijit-darwin-x64.dmg">macOS (Intel)</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://github.com/ouijit/ouijit/releases/latest/download/ouijit-linux-x64.zip">Linux (x64)</a></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On macOS, open the DMG and drag <strong>Ouijit.app</strong> to Applications. On Linux, unzip the archive and
    put the binary somewhere on your <code>PATH</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>Build from source</h3>
  <p>Building needs Node.js 20+ (the repo pins 22) and C/C++ build tools for the native modules (better-sqlite3, node-pty, koffi):</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>macOS:</strong> <code>xcode-select --install</code></li>
    <li><strong>Linux:</strong> <code>sudo apt install build-essential python3</code> (Debian/Ubuntu)</li>
  </ul>
  <pre class="glass-bevel"><code>git clone https://github.com/ouijit/ouijit.git
cd ouijit
npm install
npm start</code></pre>

  <h2>Add a project</h2>
  <p>
    On first launch you land on the <a href="/docs/terminals/">home view</a>. Click the <strong>+</strong> button in the
    sidebar and pick one of two options:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Add existing</strong>. Select a folder that is already a git repository.</li>
    <li><strong>Create new</strong>. Name a project and Ouijit creates the folder and runs <code>git init</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If the folder you add is not a git repository yet, Ouijit offers to initialize one in place, with an optional
    initial commit of the existing files.
  </p>
  <p>
    New projects are created in your <strong>projects folder</strong>, <code>~/Ouijit/projects</code> by default.
    Change it under <strong>App Settings &rarr; Projects</strong>. If projects still live in the old folder,
    Ouijit asks whether to move them (tasks, hooks, and settings stay attached), forget them (files stay on
    disk), or leave them where they are.
  </p>
  <p>Click a project in the sidebar to open it. You start on the <a href="/docs/kanban/">kanban board</a>.</p>

  <h2>Check your environment</h2>
  <p>
    Ouijit checks your environment at startup: git, the agent CLIs (<code>claude</code>, <code>codex</code>,
    <code>pi</code>, <code>opencode</code>), and the tools behind optional features (<code>gh</code>, Lima,
    nono). The <strong>Help &amp; setup</strong> dialog shows each result, and dialogs that need a missing tool
    say so inline. Starting a task with no agent CLI on <code>PATH</code> surfaces a one-time notice.
  </p>

  <h2>Update Ouijit</h2>
  <p>
    Ouijit checks for updates at launch and hourly. On macOS the update downloads in the background and a dialog
    offers to restart when it is ready. On Linux a notice links to the release page. After an update, a
    <strong>What's New</strong> dialog shows that version's release notes once.
  </p>
  <p>
    Turn automatic checks off under <strong>App Settings &rarr; Updates</strong>.
  </p>
</Docs>

